        # actually ran at (shell 26, tool 27).
        # Wave 14: the `[SPAWN][PRIO]` line of a spawn that applied its row's
        # priority as declared is debug output (`spawn-log`); both rows ask
        # for it, and so do the `linux:` rows below that wait for `row=<ELF>`
        # (static ELF, busybox sh, busybox Kconfig row and its canary,
        # pthreads): without it they could never pass, and the Kconfig-row
        # canary passed for that reason instead of its own.
        USH_SMP=1 USH_FORBID='robot> ' par_row ushell_row "sh: ctrl-c on one hart ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,spawn-log" PASS \
            "!spin
#wait spin: computing
^C
echo after-spin \$?" 120 \
            "row=TOOLBOX.ELF class=best_effort declared=27 applied=27" "stopped by an ancestor" "after-spin 130"
        # The power family's ring-3 form: `POWER.ELF`, through the
        # `power` endpoint its row grants. Rate read, set, read back;
        # suspend, which this image refuses with reason 1 (not built in): the
        # gate's config is PRESET_ROBOT and Kconfig SYSTEM_SUSPEND is never
        # on a robot image (its safety loop must keep running), so the row
        # asserts that refusal, not a resume (no build suspends today: the
        # kernel has no task freezer); an orderly reboot (the rebooted
        # shell's prompt answers the next line); an orderly power-off ends
        # QEMU.
        USH_FORBID='robot> |\[AUTHORITY\]' par_row ushell_row "sh: power tool ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat" PASS \
            "power sched_hz 200
power sched_hz
power suspend
power reboot
echo after-reboot
!power shutdown
#wait POWER\] shutdown by" 240 \
            "sched_hz set 200" "[SCHED] Scheduler rate set to 200 Hz" "sched_hz 200" \
            "[PM] suspend REFUSED: reason 1" "power: suspend refused: reason 1" "[POWER] reboot by tid" "after-reboot" \
            "[POWER] shutdown by tid"
        # Without the right to act (`power-cap-canary` leaves POWER.ELF's endpoint READ only):
        # every operation is refused by the capability, recorded (the
        # `[AUTHORITY]` line prints only when the record was handed to the
        # recorder), and does not run.
        USH_FORBID='robot> |Scheduler rate set|System suspended' par_row ushell_row "sh: power refused without the power right ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,power-cap-canary" PASS \
            "power sched_hz 200
power suspend" 150 \
            "[AUTHORITY] sched_hz set REFUSED" "[AUTHORITY] pm suspend REFUSED" "(recorded)" \
            "power: sched_hz refused" "power: suspend refused"
        # Owner decision 2026-10-23 (Q1): an orderly power-off or reboot makes
        # what was written durable on both layers. POWER.ELF asks `Power::sync`
        # first; the kernel's orderly path then syncs every volume again,
        # flushes the boot disk and shuts down its controller and the
        # kernel-held devices (`[ORDERLY]` lines, in that order). The file is
        # written, the machine powered off through the service, and boot 2 (a
        # second QEMU on the same volume) reads it back (`wc`: the counts are
        # not in the typed line). The reboot row is the same path through
        # `Power::reboot` on one QEMU. No canary here: the time the shell takes
        # to start POWER.ELF is longer than FS_WRITEBACK_MAX_AGE_MS on a loaded
        # host, so `fs-wb` may write the file first (measured 2026-10-23: the
        # sync-skip canary kept the file). The deterministic canary is the
        # kernel smoke's (`power-off: the kernel's sync keeps a written file`).
        # QEMU's virtio-blk cannot tell the device half (its cache is the
        # host's page cache, which a power-off keeps): `[ORDERLY] disk:` is
        # asserted printed, not its effect.
        ush_poweroff_row() { # ush_poweroff_row <label (isa)> <rv|arm> <features>
            local label="$1" isa="$2" feats="$3"
            local img="$CI_LOG_DIR/ush-poweroff-$isa.img"
            mkdir -p "$CI_LOG_DIR"; rm -f "$img"
            USH_IMG="$img" USH_FORBID='robot> |\[AUTHORITY\]' \
                ushell_row "$label, boot 1" "$isa" "$feats" PASS \
                "#wait \[OTA\] Boot marked good
!echo durable-ok > /fat/DUR.TXT
!power shutdown
#wait \[POWER\] shutdown by" 240 \
                "[POWER] sync by tid" "[ORDERLY] filesystems: " "[ORDERLY] disk: flush " "[POWER] shutdown by tid"
            # Boot 2 boots the kernel boot 1 was built and cloned for: after
            # its par_ready a parallel job may not build (`par_shared`).
            local k2="$KERNEL"; [ "$isa" = rv ] || k2="$A64_IMG"
            USH_KERNEL="$k2" USH_IMG="$img" USH_FORBID='robot> ' ushell_row "$label" "$isa" "$feats" PASS \
                "wc < /fat/DUR.TXT" 120 \
                "1 1 11"
            rm -f "$img"
        }
        par_row ush_poweroff_row "sh: power-off keeps a written file ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat"
        USH_FORBID='robot> |\[AUTHORITY\]' par_row ushell_row "sh: reboot keeps a written file ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat" PASS \
            "echo durable-ok > /fat/DUR.TXT
power reboot
wc < /fat/DUR.TXT" 240 \
            "[POWER] sync by tid" "power: filesystems synced" "[ORDERLY] filesystems: " "[ORDERLY] disk: flush " \
            "[POWER] reboot by tid" "1 1 11"
        # Wave 12: the other privileged families' tools (`FLIGHT.ELF`,
        # `BEHAVIOR.ELF`, `CONFIG.ELF`, `OTA.ELF`), each through the
        # endpoint its row grants, running the console's
        # own command bodies; and `ps` (TOOLBOX.ELF) over the introspection
        # tree's admin task list. Only
        # reversible operations: arm then disarm, a layer off then on, a key
        # set then read back, the status and a rollback that a fresh volume
        # answers "already on the last good slot". `ps` holds the full task
        # view (`Cap<Task>` READ on "tasks", owner round 48), so it lists a
        # task that is not its own descendant: an idle task (PPID 0, PRI 31,
        # running), whatever its TID: since the camera's capture task (B2)
        # is created first, the idle tasks no longer start at TID 1.
        # Without the grant its row is refused the admin region and `ps`
        # lists nothing.
        # `flight-tool-drivetrain`
        # gives the drivetrain to FLIGHT.ELF (the default topology gives it to
        # the autorun row: one writer per motor).
        USH_FORBID='robot> |REFUSED|command not found' par_row ushell_row "sh: family tools ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,use-robot,flight-tool-drivetrain" PASS \
            "flight arm
flight disarm
behavior disable 2
behavior status
behavior enable 2
config set log_level 2
config get log_level
ota status
ota rollback
ps" 240 \
            "[FLIGHT] flight arm by tid" "flight: arm ok" "[FLIGHT] DISARMED" "flight: disarm ok" \
            "behavior: L2 disabled" "behavior: enabled layers L0 L1 L3" "behavior: L2 enabled" \
            "[CFG] set by tid" "log_level=2" "ota: active A last-good A" \
            "ota: already on the last good slot" "  TID  PPID PRI S NAME" " TOOLBOX.ELF" " R inspect" \
            "     0  31 R idle"
        # Canary half (`family-cap-canary` leaves each tool's endpoint READ only):
        # every operation refused by the missing right, recorded (the `[AUTHORITY]`
        # line prints only when the record reached the recorder), not run.
        USH_FORBID='robot> |FLIGHT\] ARMED|CFG\] set by|behavior: L2|ota: active' par_row ushell_row "sh: family tools refused without their capability ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,use-robot,family-cap-canary" PASS \
            "flight arm
behavior disable 2
config set log_level 2
ota rollback" 150 \
            "[AUTHORITY] flight arm REFUSED" "[AUTHORITY] behavior disable REFUSED" \
            "[AUTHORITY] config set REFUSED" "[AUTHORITY] ota rollback REFUSED" "(recorded)" \
            "flight: arm refused" "ota: rollback refused"
        # `exit 1` ends the shell and, with its status, the terminal server (the
        # console program) with it; the component manager restarts both, the
        # server first (`exited: restarting` is the kernel's line for each
        # death, so the restart path is real: three restarts show it). The
        # fourth failure exhausts both bursts in one reap
        # (`SUP_RESTART_BURST` = 3 restarts): nothing takes the console within
        # `SH_START_TIMEOUT_S` of that death (`did not take the console
        # within`), and the recovery console does. Either line ends in
        # `: recovery console`; the boot's `pending (recovery console after`
        # line does not match it.
        par_row ushell_row "sh: supervisor gives up, recovery console ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat" PASS \
            "exit 1
exit 1
exit 1
!exit 1
#wait robot> " 180 \
            "exited: restarting" "[compmgr] restart SH.ELF" "[compmgr] give-up TERMD.ELF" ": recovery console" "robot> "
        # ── The Linux personality (wave 12) ──────────────────────
        # `LXHELLO.ELF` is a static Linux ELF (`userspace/tests/lxhello`, our
        # own C, no C library) run from the shell under its row's
        # `abi = "linux"` (`linux-abi-test`). It checks its initial stack
        # (argv, env, auxv), identity, time and sleep, brk and mmap, the tty
        # ioctls, signal state, a pipe through dup3, a file (fstat, read,
        # lseek), newfstatat, getdents64 over /fat, writev, wait4, fcntl,
        # and prints `lx: done failures=0`. The capability canary is inside
        # it: its row holds nothing, so `openat(O_CREAT)` under /fat must be
        # refused by the native tree gate AND recorded (`(recorded)` prints
        # only when the record reached the recorder). A call it does not
        # answer gets `-ENOSYS`, reported, never a native call.
        # Canaries: `linux-abi-caps-canary` grants the row `/fat` read-write,
        # so the create succeeds and the refusal lines are missing;
        # `linux-abi-tag-canary` never tags the task, so its Linux numbers hit
        # the native table and seccomp kills it at its first call.
        USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> |lx: .* FAIL|SECCOMP' par_row ushell_row "linux: static ELF under the personality ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,spawn-log" PASS \
            "lxhello one 'two words'
echo after-lx \$?" 120 \
            "row=LXHELLO.ELF" "lx: hello from a static Linux ELF, argc=3 [lxhello] [one] [two words]" \
            "lx: auxv ok" "lx: pipe ok" "lx: file ok" "lx: getdents64 ok" "lx: writev ok" \
            "[LINUX] tid=" "openat refused by the capability check: -EACCES (recorded)" \
            "lx: create outside caps refused ok" "not answered by the personality: -ENOSYS" \
            "lx: fork+wait4 ok" "lx: fp state kept across switches ok" "lx: thread pointer kept across switches ok" \
            "lx: fork child holds only its row ok" \
            "lx: inherited file reads [ab]" "lx: fork shares the file offset ok" \
            "lx: exec child argc=2 ok" "lx: execve ok" \
            "lx: execve: the new image starts with every register zero but sp ok" \
            "lx: signal handler ok" "lx: sigreturn restores fp ok" "lx: signal mask ok" "lx: sigchld ok" \
            "lx: default action ok" "lx: sigpipe ok" "lx: kill a child: eintr + handler ok" \
            "lx: kill the parent refused ok" "lx: signal delivered at an interrupt ok" \
            "lx: wait4 reports a signalled child WIFSIGNALED ok" \
            "lx: orphan adopted by its subreaper ok" \
            "lx: mprot: a store to PROT_READ memory faults ok" "lx: mprot: mprotect(PROT_READ) makes a store fault ok" \
            "lx: mprot: mprotect(PROT_EXEC) is refused ok" "lx: mprot: a PROT_NONE mapping made read-write ok" \
            "lx: done failures=0" "after-lx 0"
        # D6 (Kconfig LINUX_RUNTIME, `crates/core/abi/src/foreign.rs`): the same
        # personality hosted by the ring-3 runtime (`userspace/lib/linux_rt`),
        # which the kernel maps into LXHELLO's address space at spawn and which
        # answers each Linux call the kernel reflects to it. `lxhello rt` runs
        # the calls the runtime answers over the native ABI today (the
        # in-kernel personality answers every one of them too, so the stage
        # compares the two by cost, not by answer); the full `lxhello` run stays
        # on the in-kernel rows above until the runtime answers seek,
        # mprotect, signals, threads and exec. `[SPAWN] ... hosted by the
        # ring-3 runtime` is printed only for a hosted child (`spawn-log`).
        # `lxhello spawn` then runs three spawns whose request names more than
        # the console: a working directory (`cd /fat`: the runtime's `getcwd`
        # must say `/fat`, its default is `/`), a pipe on descriptor 0, and a
        # file on descriptor 0 with another on descriptor 1.
        # Canaries: the same script on the in-kernel kernel (the hosted line is
        # missing), and on a runtime kernel built with `hosted-startup-canary`
        # (no startup block: `/`, console input, output on the console).
        # Either must fail the row.
        lx_rt_markers=("hosted by the ring-3 runtime" "lx: rt: getpid ok" "lx: rt: uname ok" "lx: rt: getcwd ok"
            "lx: rt: umask ok" "lx: rt: nanosleep ok" "lx: rt: brk ok" "lx: rt: mmap ok" "lx: rt: ioctl tty ok"
            "lx: rt: signal state ok" "lx: rt: pipe ok" "lx: rt: file ok" "lx: rt: newfstatat ok"
            "lx: rt: getdents64 ok" "lx: writev ok" "lx: rt: wait4 ok" "lx: rt: create outside caps refused ok"
            "lx: rt: unanswered call ok" "lx: fork+wait4 ok" "lx: fp state kept across switches ok"
            "lx: thread pointer kept across switches ok" "lx: fork child holds only its row ok"
            "lx: fork shares the file offset ok" "lx: bench getpid x1000 ns=" "lx: bench read x1000 ns="
            "lx: rt done failures=0"
            "lx: spawn cwd=/fat" "lx: spawn stdin kind=fifo" "lx: spawn stdin=[piped-ok]"
            "lx: spawn stdin kind=file" "lx: spawn stdin=[from-a-file]" "lx: spawn done")
        lx_rt_script="lxhello rt
cd /fat
echo piped-ok | lxhello spawn
echo from-a-file > /fat/RTIN.TXT
lxhello spawn < /fat/RTIN.TXT > /fat/RTOUT.TXT
cat /fat/RTOUT.TXT"
        lx_rt_cfg="$(cd "$CI_LOG_DIR" && pwd)/lxrt-${ush_isa}.config"
        lx_rt_k="$CI_LOG_DIR/kernel-lxrt-${ush_isa}"
        lx_rt_kc="$CI_LOG_DIR/kernel-lxrt-canary-${ush_isa}"
        if [ "$ush_isa" = rv ]; then
            cp "${REPO_ROOT}/config/defconfigs/${GATE_DEFC_RV}.config" "$lx_rt_cfg"
        else
            cp "${REPO_ROOT}/config/defconfigs/${GATE_DEFC_A64}.config" "$lx_rt_cfg"
        fi
        printf 'CONFIG_LINUX_RUNTIME=y\n' >>"$lx_rt_cfg"
        if (cd "$REPO_ROOT" && KCONFIG_CONFIG="$lx_rt_cfg" python3 -m olddefconfig >/dev/null 2>&1) \
           && grep -q '^CONFIG_LINUX_RUNTIME=y$' "$lx_rt_cfg" \
           && make -s "$([ "$ush_isa" = rv ] && echo $AZOS_OUT/build/linux_rt.elf || echo $AZOS_OUT/build/aarch64/linux_rt.elf)" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
           && if [ "$ush_isa" = rv ]; then
                  KCONFIG_CONFIG="$lx_rt_cfg" kbuild "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,spawn-log" && cp "$KERNEL" "$lx_rt_k" \
                      && KCONFIG_CONFIG="$lx_rt_cfg" kbuild "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,spawn-log,hosted-startup-canary" && cp "$KERNEL" "$lx_rt_kc"
              else
                  CI_KDEFER=0 AARCH64_CONFIG="$lx_rt_cfg" a64_kbuild "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,spawn-log" && cp "$A64_IMG" "$lx_rt_k" \
                      && CI_KDEFER=0 AARCH64_CONFIG="$lx_rt_cfg" a64_kbuild "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,spawn-log,hosted-startup-canary" && cp "$A64_IMG" "$lx_rt_kc"
              fi; then
            USH_KERNEL="$lx_rt_k" USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> |lx: .* FAIL|SECCOMP' par_row ushell_row "linux: hosted by the ring-3 runtime ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,spawn-log" PASS \
                "$lx_rt_script" 150 "${lx_rt_markers[@]}"
            USH_KERNEL="$lx_rt_kc" USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> ' par_row ushell_row "linux: hosted spawn canary, no startup block ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,spawn-log,hosted-startup-canary" FAIL \
                "$lx_rt_script" 150 "${lx_rt_markers[@]}"
            # The fork child's descriptors and capabilities (wave 13, NATFORK;
            # `crates/core/syscall/src/natfork.rs`) are the hosted fork's now:
            # a native process has no fork of its own, and the runtime copies
            # its process with `SYS_FOREIGN_FORK`. A child inherits its
            # parent's files and pipe ends as duplicates sharing the open
            # description, at the parent's handles, and holds the capabilities
            # of its OWN row, never a copy of the parent's table
            # (`lx: fork shares the file offset ok`, `lx: fork child holds
            # only its row ok`). Canaries: `native-fork-no-inherit-canary` (no
            # descriptor inherited) and, for the per-row seed template, an entry
            # that gains WRITE after it was sealed (LXHELLO's read-only root).
            # The first fork of the row records the template and the second,
            # lxhello's authority fork, is seeded from it:
            # `natfork-template-noseal-canary` serves the edited entry (that
            # child's create under /fat is not refused with EACCES: `lx: fork
            # child holds only its row FAIL`), `natfork-template-tamper-canary`
            # has the seal refuse it (dropped, minted again, said once: the run
            # is green and the line is there). `native-fork-copy-canary` (the
            # parent's other handles kept) is the ipctest row `fork: stale
            # parent handles canary`: lxhello's parent holds nothing a
            # placeholder would keep.
            for nfc in native-fork-no-inherit-canary natfork-template-noseal-canary natfork-template-tamper-canary; do
                lx_rt_kn="$CI_LOG_DIR/kernel-lxrt-${nfc}-${ush_isa}"
                if [ "$ush_isa" = rv ]; then
                    KCONFIG_CONFIG="$lx_rt_cfg" kbuild "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,spawn-log,$nfc" && cp "$KERNEL" "$lx_rt_kn"
                else
                    CI_KDEFER=0 AARCH64_CONFIG="$lx_rt_cfg" a64_kbuild "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,spawn-log,$nfc" && cp "$A64_IMG" "$lx_rt_kn"
                fi || { printf "  %-26s" "linux: hosted fork canary $nfc ($ush_isa)..."; bad; echo "      the kernel with $nfc did not build"; continue; }
                if [ "$nfc" = natfork-template-tamper-canary ]; then
                    USH_KERNEL="$lx_rt_kn" USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> |lx: .* FAIL|SECCOMP' par_row ushell_row "linux: hosted fork canary $nfc ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,spawn-log,$nfc" PASS \
                        "$lx_rt_script" 150 "${lx_rt_markers[@]}" "capability template failed its seal: dropped, minted again"
                elif [ "$nfc" = natfork-template-noseal-canary ]; then
                    # Red for its own reason: the template-seeded child's check.
                    USH_KERNEL="$lx_rt_kn" USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> |SECCOMP' par_row ushell_row "linux: hosted fork canary $nfc ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,spawn-log,$nfc" PASS \
                        "$lx_rt_script" 150 "hosted by the ring-3 runtime" "lx: fork shares the file offset ok" "lx: fork child holds only its row FAIL"
                else
                    USH_KERNEL="$lx_rt_kn" USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> ' par_row ushell_row "linux: hosted fork canary $nfc ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,spawn-log,$nfc" FAIL \
                        "$lx_rt_script" 150 "${lx_rt_markers[@]}"
                fi
            done
        else
            printf "  %-26s" "linux: hosted by the ring-3 runtime ($ush_isa)..."
            bad; echo "      the LINUX_RUNTIME kernel did not build"
        fi
        USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> ' par_row ushell_row "linux: runtime canary, in-kernel personality ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,spawn-log" FAIL \
            "$lx_rt_script" 150 "${lx_rt_markers[@]}"
        USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> ' par_row ushell_row "linux: caps canary, row granted /fat ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,linux-abi-caps-canary" FAIL \
            "lxhello
echo after-lx" 120 \
            "openat refused by the capability check" "lx: create outside caps refused ok" "lx: done failures=0"
        USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> ' par_row ushell_row "linux: tag canary, native table ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,linux-abi-tag-canary" FAIL \
            "lxhello
echo after-lx" 120 \
            "lx: hello from a static Linux ELF" "lx: done failures=0"
        # Shared open file descriptions (round 48): a descriptor inherited
        # across fork, or dup'ed, shares one offset with its original, as in
        # Linux. LXHELLO's child writes "a" to an inherited /tmp descriptor,
        # the parent then writes "b"; the file must read "ab" (the main row
        # above). Canary `fd-private-offset-canary`: every dup gets a private
        # copy of the offset, the parent overwrites the child's byte and the
        # file reads "b".
        # Wave 13 (security): LXHELLO's `mprot:` checks (a store to PROT_READ
        # memory, and to memory `mprotect` made read-only, faults; PROT_EXEC is
        # refused; a PROT_NONE mapping is made usable). Canary
        # `mmap-prot-canary`: read-write mappings, `mprotect` a no-op.
        USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> ' par_row ushell_row "linux: mmap prot canary ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,mmap-prot-canary" FAIL \
            "lxhello
echo after-lx" 150 \
            "lx: mprot: a store to PROT_READ memory faults ok" "lx: mprot: mprotect(PROT_READ) makes a store fault ok"
        # The exec hand-off: LXHELLO's exec child execs with a pattern in
        # every register but sp, the arguments and the number (aarch64: in
        # TPIDR_EL0 too), and its exec'd image must find all of them zero at
        # `_start` (exits 8 otherwise). Canary `exec-regs-canary`: the hand-off
        # writes only pc/status/sp and keeps the thread pointer, as before.
        USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> ' par_row ushell_row "linux: exec regs canary, old image's registers kept ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,linux-abi-test,exec-regs-canary" FAIL \
            "lxhello
echo after-lx" 150 \
            "lx: execve: the new image starts with every register zero but sp ok"
        USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> ' par_row ushell_row "linux: shared offset canary ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,fd-private-offset-canary" FAIL \
            "lxhello
echo after-lx" 150 \
            "lx: inherited file reads [ab]" "lx: fork shares the file offset ok"
        # Stage 3 canaries. `linux-abi-fork-canary` also grants a forked child
        # `/fat` read-write, which its row does not declare: the child's create
        # succeeds and the reseed marker is missing. `linux-abi-fp-canary`
        # stops a Linux task's per-task state following it: on riscv64 its FP
        # file, on aarch64 its TPIDR_EL0 (the FP file there is the kernel's
        # own lazy switch). With one hart the parent and its fork child, which
        # set different values, clobber each other's.
        USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> ' par_row ushell_row "linux: fork canary, child beyond its row ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,linux-abi-fork-canary" FAIL \
            "lxhello
echo after-lx" 150 \
            "lx: fork child holds only its row ok"
        if [ "$ush_isa" = rv ]; then
            USH_SMP=1 USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> ' par_row ushell_row "linux: fp canary, file not switched (rv)" rv "$ush_feat,linux-abi-fp-canary" FAIL \
                "lxhello
echo after-lx" 150 \
                "lx: fp state kept across switches ok"
        else
            USH_SMP=1 USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> ' par_row ushell_row "linux: tls canary, TPIDR_EL0 not switched (arm)" arm "$ush_feat,linux-abi-fp-canary" FAIL \
                "lxhello
echo after-lx" 150 \
                "lx: thread pointer kept across switches ok"
        fi
        # Wave 13 (SIGNALS): the main row above runs LXHELLO's signal checks
        # (a handler at kill's return with siginfo, the FP file and every
        # register back after rt_sigreturn, a mask holding a signal pending,
        # SIGCHLD at a child's exit, a default action ending a task with
        # 128 + signo, SIGPIPE ignored then default, a parent's signal ending
        # a child's sleep with -EINTR and running its handler, a child refused
        # signalling its parent, and delivery at an interrupt to a task that
        # makes no syscall). Canaries, each compiling one property out:
        # nothing delivered; no SIGCHLD; no SIGPIPE.
        USH_DISK=lxabi USH_FORBID='robot> ' par_row ushell_row "linux: signal canary, nothing delivered ($ush_isa)" "$ush_isa" "$ush_feat,linux-signal-canary" FAIL \
            "lxhello
echo after-lx" 150 \
